s \"research subagent\" and \"think subagent\" references, but not trigger phrases matching regex — \"research subagent\" is lowercase, and \"research subagent\" doesn't match \"派发 subagent\" etc). Wait, let me check. `skills/sense.md` has \"research subagent 大量调研\" and \"think subagent\". These don't match the regex (they're not \"派发 subagent\" or \"Main/Sub Agent\"). So they're not counted. But semantically they're natural language references to subagents. The task focuses on trigger words though.\n\nLet me also check skills/research.md, skills/exporter.md, skills/debug_skill.md (review debug agent), skills/import_ctx.md.\n\nFor the migration, my priority is:\n1. Migrate the trigger-word-bearing files to reduce the count (required for Test 1).\n2. Add explicit pi syntax to at least 1 file (required for Test 1 pi_files >= 1).\n3. Add agent names to at least 1 file (required for Test 2).\n4. Preserve SENSE semantics in sense_loop.md (required for Test 3).\n\nNow, the important subtlety: The `workflowScript` examples will contain `agent:'think'` etc. But I also need to be careful NOT to reintroduce trigger phrases. When I write \"SPAWN Sub Agent\" → I replace with \"runs.run(...)\" syntax.\n\nLet me now design the actual migration content for each file.\n\n### Design principles\n\nI'll add a \"触发语法\" (trigger syntax) section to relevant templates, using pi's explicit syntax. I'll write it in a way that:\n- Uses `workflowScript` + `runs.run`/`runs.all`\n- Uses real agent names `agent:'worker'/'reviewer'/'think'/'research'/'exporter'`\n- No `{{` in the content (avoid `${...}` with double braces — actually `${}` is fine, but let me minimize risk by using simple task strings without interpolation where possible, or use `+` concatenation like the pi examples do `\"Review correctness: \" + scan.output`)\n- Uses backticks for template literals (fine in .md)\n\nActually, wait — I need to be careful about `${}`. The extractVariables only matches `{{`. `${` = `$` + `{`, single