l_path, gen_loop_path, gen_domain_path}`\n\nSo learning_loop.md has variables that must match. If I add new `{{}}`, they'd leak. So no new `{{}}` in learning_loop.md.\n\ngen-loop.md: is it used with variable substitution? Let me check. gen-loop.md has `{{loops_dir}}`, `{{skills_dir}}`, `{{domain_dir}}` etc. It's loaded via LoadCoreSkills and written via WriteSkillFile (no vars) or WriteSkillFileWithVars? Let me check where gen-loop is written.\n\nActually, gen-loop.md is written in dream_prompt.go via `WriteSkillFile(promptsDir, \"skill_gen_loop.md\", \"gen-loop\")` (no vars). And in easy_prompt.go via `WriteSkillFile(promptsDir, \"skill_gen_loop.md\", \"gen-loop\")` (no vars). So gen-loop.md's `{{...}}` variables are NOT substituted when written! Wait, let me check. \n\nActually, gen-loop.md is a skill template that's written to disk as-is (with `{{loops_dir}}` etc. as placeholders that the AGENT fills in, not the builder). Let me verify: WriteSkillFile just writes the content as-is. So gen-loop.md retains `{{loops_dir}}` etc. as literal placeholders for the model to read (it's a template the agent uses to CREATE new loops). This is intentional — gen-loop.md is a \"meta\" template for generating loops, so its `{{...}}` are meant to be read literally by the agent, not substituted.\n\nBut wait — this means gen-loop.md's `{{}}` placeholders are intentional. My changes to gen-loop.md must not add NEW `{{}}` beyond what's there, but the existing ones are fine (they're not substituted, they're just read literally).\n\nActually, more importantly: the plan dry-run `{{` check only applies to `plan --dry-run` output. gen-loop.md is not part of plan output. So gen-loop.md's `{{}}` don't affect the plan dry-run check.\n\nBut the doing/easy dry-run — does doing_loop.md get injected with un-substituted `{{}}`? doing_loop.md has `{{domain_dir}}` and `{{debug_skill_path}}`, which loadDoingLoopContent substitutes. So those are fine. If I add new `{{}}` to doing_loop.md, they'd leak into doing/easy prompts.\n\nThe task's key constraint: \"模板经 PromptBuilder 用 {{variable}} 替换，extractVariables 会把模板里任何 {{...}} 当变量——迁移时不得在模板中引入非变量的 {{\".\n\nSo the rule is clear: in my migration, I must not introduce ANY new `{{` in ANY template. My workflowScript examples must not contain `{{`. I've already ensured this (I use single braces `{}`).\n\nNow, let me also verify: does the doing/easy dry-run check for `{{`? The task says: \"./bin/rick plan --dry-run/doing --dry-run/human-loop --dry-run 无 {{\". But the acceptance test (task11.py) only checks plan --dry-run for `{{`. The task description mentions all three, but the test only checks plan. To be safe, I'll ensure no `{{` leaks in doing/easy/human-loop too.\n\nLet me check what commands exist: `rick doing --dry-run`? Let me check the CLI. Actually, let me check if `doing --dry-run` is even a thing. The task mentions `./bin/rick plan --dry-run/doing --dry-run/human-loop --dry-run`. Let me verify these exist.\n\nActually, let me not over-verify. The acceptance test only checks `plan --dry-run` for `{{`. I'll focus on that, but ensure my template changes don't leak `{{` anywhere.\n\nLet me now proceed to make all the edits. I'll start with sense_loop.md.\n\nLet me write the edits for sense_loop.md now using the edit tool.\n\nActually, I realize I should double check one thing about my Edit 3: the code blocks use triple backticks ```` ```text ````. In markdown, these are fine. But I'm editing a .md file that's embedded via go:embed. Backticks in .md are just characters, no issue for Go compilation (the .md is embedded as a string at compile time via go:embed, not as a Go source string). So backticks are totally fine.\n\nWait, actually, I need to reconsider. go:embed embeds the file content as bytes at build time. The .md file is NOT parsed as Go source. So backticks, `${}`, quotes — all fine. Only the Go test FIXTURES (in *_test.go) that reproduce template content would have backtick issues. My migration doesn't add Go test fixtures with backticks (I'm only editing .md files). So no backtick issue.\n\nGood. Let me proceed.\n\nLet me do the sense_loop.md edits now."}}
