ck-gates/helper.py <doing_dir>` 校验\n   （tasks.json 可解析 / 无 zombie running / success 有 commit_hash）。\n```\n\nSo I need to `git add` + `git commit` with task ID. Then the rick-gates helper.py checks that the task's commit_hash is set in tasks.json.\n\nBut wait — the tasks.json commit_hash is set by the rick automation (the parent doing orchestrator), NOT by me. Let me understand the flow:\n- The `rick doing` command spawns me (pi) to do task11.\n- I do the work and commit.\n- The rick automation captures my commit hash and updates tasks.json.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the mark_task_success skill and the flow, the agent (me) does the commit, and then the rick harness (helper.py) verifies.\n\nLet me look at how tasks.json and the commit flow works. Let me check the current tasks.json and the mark_task_success skill.\n\nActually, let me step back. The key deliverable is done (template migration + tests pass). Now I need to:\n1. Commit my changes.\n2. Run doing_check.\n\nFor the commit, let me be careful about scope. I'll commit ONLY:\n- `internal/prompt/templates/` (all my template changes)\n- `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt`\n\nI should NOT commit:\n- `.rick/domain/project-conventions.md` (not mine — wait, let me check if this is actually from a previous task that forgot to commit)\n- `.rick/dream/prompts/*` (generated, stale)\n- `.rick/loops/tdd-red-green-refactor-loop.md` (not mine)\n- `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/tasks.json` (runtime state, handled by harness)\n- `bin/rick` (build artifact — but convention shows it's committed per task)\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Let me look at what the previous tasks actually committed to understand the pattern. Let me check the git show for a previous \"feat\" commit.\n\nActually, let me check what files are in the last \"feat(task10)\" commit vs \"chore(task10): rebuild bin/rick\"."}}
