{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"- `.gitignore` ignores `bin/` — so `bin/rick` is NOT tracked (it was showing as \"M\" earlier? wait, that's confusing). Let me re-check. Actually, `git status` showed `bin/rick` as \"M\" (modified). But `.gitignore` has `bin/`. \n\nWait, this is contradictory. If `bin/` is gitignored, then `bin/rick` shouldn't show in git status. Unless `bin/rick` was force-added to git before the gitignore rule, or the gitignore `bin/` doesn't match `bin/rick`?\n\nActually, `bin/` in gitignore matches the `bin` directory and everything under it. But if `bin/rick` was already tracked (committed) before the gitignore was added, git continues to track it. Let me check.\n\nFrom the git log, there are commits \"chore(taskN): rebuild bin/rick\" with `bin/rick | Bin ...`. So `bin/rick` IS tracked (committed despite gitignore, because it was force-added or added before gitignore).\n\nHmm wait, but `git check-ignore .rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt` returned exit=1 (not ignored). And `.rick/jobs/job_35/` is untracked (shows as `??`).\n\nLet me check the full picture. Actually, the `bin/` in .gitignore — but `bin/rick` is committed. This means either:\n1. `bin/rick` was added with `git add -f` (force), OR\n2. The gitignore was added AFTER bin/rick was already tracked.\n\nEither way, `bin/rick` is tracked and shows as modified.\n\nOK, this is getting into the weeds. Let me focus on what matters for the task:\n\n1. My template changes are done and verified (acceptance test passes).\n2. I need to commit my changes.\n3. I need to run doing_check.\n\nFor the commit, the cleanest scope for `feat(task11)`:\n- `internal/prompt/templates/` (all my template changes)\n\nThe `trigger-baseline.txt` is a required artifact per the task's test method (\"迁移前先...落盘\"). But it's in `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/` which is a runtime directory. Should I commit it? The test method says to write it there, and task11.py reads it from there. It's part of the job's runtime state. I think it should NOT be committed to the main repo (it's job-specific runtime data). But the acceptance test reads it from disk, so it just needs to exist on disk (which it does).\n\nActually, re-reading the task: \"迁移前先以验收同一正则捕获自然语言触发词基线计数并落盘（如 `.rick/jobs/job_35/doing/trigger-baseline.txt`）\". This is a runtime artifact for the test to compare against. It just needs to exist on disk. I've created it. Whether it's committed is unclear, but since it's in `.rick/jobs/` (runtime), I'll leave it uncommitted (the harness manages `.rick/jobs/`).\n\nSo my commit scope: `internal/prompt/templates/` only.\n\nLet me now commit the template changes. Then rebuild bin/rick and commit it (following the convention).\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider the bin/rick rebuild. The convention shows \"chore(taskN): rebuild bin/rick\" as a SEPARATE commit. This is because the templates are embedded into the binary via go:embed, so the binary needs rebuilding to reflect template changes.\n\nSo my plan:\n1. `git add internal/prompt/templates/` + commit as `feat(task11): ...`\n2. `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick` (already done) + `git add bin/rick` + commit as `chore(task11): rebuild bin/rick`\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should commit bin/rick. The `bin/` is gitignored but bin/rick is tracked (committed). The convention clearly rebuilds and commits it. So yes.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. If I do `git add bin/rick`, git might complain it's ignored. But since it's already tracked, `git add bin/rick` works on tracked files. Let me verify.\n\nActually, let me just do the commit for templates first, then handle bin/rick.\n\nLet me also double-check: are there any OTHER tracked files I modified that are mine? From git status:\n- `internal/prompt/templates/*` (mine) ✓\n- `bin/rick` (mine, rebuilt) ✓\n\nThe others (.rick/domain, .rick/dream/prompts, .rick/loops, .rick/jobs/doing/tasks.json) are NOT mine. They were modified by previous tasks or the harness.\n\nWait, but actually, `.rick/dream/prompts/dream_prompt.md`, `skill_gen_loop.md`, `skill_sense.md` — these are generated from templates. Since I changed templates (gen-loop.md), the generated `skill_gen_loop.md` is now stale. But I didn't modify sense.md, so why is `skill_sense.md` modified?\n\nLet me check: did a previous task (task10 or task8) modify these? The commit \"feat(task10): pibuilder 单文件内聚 — 内联关键 skill/loop 内容\" changed pibuilder.go to inline skill/loop content. This might have changed how dream prompts are generated, hence the stale `.rick/dream/prompts/*`.\n\nActually, these `.rick/dream/prompts/*` files are RUNTIME artifacts. They're tracked (committed previously) but they're stale now. This is a pre-existing issue from previous tasks, not my concern.\n\nHmm, but actually — wait. Let me check if these `.rick/dream/prompts/*` files were modified by MY actions. I ran `./bin/rick dream --dry-run` which should NOT write files (dry-run). And `go build` doesn't write .rick/dream/prompts. So these were modified BEFORE I started (by previous tasks).\n\nLet me verify by checking the git diff of one of them to see if it's related to my changes.\n\nActually, I'm overanalyzing. Let me just:\n1. Verify my changes are correct (done — acceptance test passes).\n2. Commit my template changes.\n3. Rebuild + commit bin/rick.\n4. Run doing_check.\n\nThe other modified files (.rick/*) are from previous tasks and I should leave them alone (not revert, not commit).\n\nLet me commit now. First, let me stage only my template changes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time whether I need to worry about the `.rick/dream/prompts/*` files. The doing_check gate (helper.py) might check for consistency. Let me look at what doing_check does.\n\nActually, let me just run doing_check and see if it passes. That's the ultimate gate.\n\nLet me first commit my template changes, then run doing_check.\n\nLet me write a good commit mes