{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Interesting — `bin/rick` is actually gitignored (by `bin/` in .gitignore), but it was showing as \"M\" in git status earlier. Wait, that's contradictory.\n\nLet me check: git status showed `M bin/rick` earlier. But now `git add bin/rick` says it's ignored. \n\nLet me investigate. Maybe `bin/rick` was tracked before and `.gitignore` was added later, OR there's a `.git/info/exclude` or something. Actually, if a file is already tracked, `git add` on it should work (gitignore only affects UNtracked files). So the \"ignored\" error means `bin/rick` is NOT actually tracked.\n\nWait, but git status showed `M bin/rick`. Let me re-check with `git ls-files bin/rick`.\n\nActually, looking back at my earlier `git status --short` output, it showed:\n```\n M bin/rick\n```\n\nHmm, but that might have been a misread. Let me check `git ls-files` to see if bin/rick is tracked."}}
