{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Weird. `bin/rick` is tracked (git ls-files shows it), but `git add bin/rick` says it's ignored. And `git check-ignore bin/rick` returns exit 1.\n\nThis is likely because `git add` with a pathspec `bin/rick`... hmm. Actually, wait — maybe the issue is that `bin/rick` is tracked but git add is treating the whole `bin` directory as ignored because of some interaction.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The previous commits \"chore(taskN): rebuild bin/rick\" show `bin/rick | Bin`. So bin/rick WAS committed before. It's tracked. But now `git add bin/rick` fails with \"ignored\".\n\nThis might be because a PREVIOUS commit REMOVED bin/rick from tracking (e.g., `git rm --cached bin/rick`), making it untracked again, and now gitignore applies.\n\nBut `git ls-files bin/rick` shows it IS tracked. Contradiction.\n\nWait, let me look again. Maybe there's a case issue or the file was deleted and recreated.\n\nActually, let me check `git status` for bin/rick more carefully — is it \"M\" (modified) or \" D\" (deleted) or \"??\" (untracked)?\n\nFrom `git status --short bin/`, it showed `M  bin/rick`. So it's \"M\" (modified, tracked).\n\nHmm, but `git add` says ignored. This is a known git quirk: if a file is tracked but also matches a gitignore pattern, `git add` on it... actually no, tracked files are NOT subject to gitignore for `git add` (git add updates the index for tracked files regardless of gitignore).\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that the gitignore `bin/` has a trailing issue, OR there's a `.git/info/exclude`.\n\nActually, let me just try `git add -f bin/rick` to force it. But wait, if bin/rick is already tracked and modified, I shouldn't need -f.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. Let me check `git diff bin/rick` and `git diff --cached bin/rick` to understand its state.\n\nActually, this is a rabbit hole. The bin/rick rebuild is a nice-to-have (follows convention) but not strictly required for the task. The template changes are the deliverable and are committed.\n\nLet me check: is bin/rick currently in a consistent state? The build I did (`go build -o bin/rick`) updated it with my template changes. But whether it's committed or not doesn't affect the task's acceptance (the acceptance test uses `go build` to verify compilation, and `./bin/rick plan --dry-run` uses the on-disk binary).\n\nActually wait — the acceptance test task11.py uses `RICK_BIN = os.path.join(PROJECT_ROOT, 'bin', 'rick')` and runs `[RICK_BIN, 'plan', '--dry-run']`. So it uses the on-disk `bin/rick` binary. I rebuilt it with `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick`, so it's up-to-date. Good.\n\nThe commit of bin/rick is just a repo hygiene thing (following the convention of previous tasks). But since `git add bin/rick` fails with \"ignored\", it seems the current state is different from before.\n\nLet me investigate briefly: why is bin/rick tracked (ls-files) but git add says ignored?\n\nActually, let me check the git index state for bin/rick."}}
