{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I see clearly:\n- `M  bin/rick` (staged for commit, \"M \" means staged modification). \n- `git rev-parse HEAD:bin/rick` = `6e2a376c...` (the HEAD version)\n- `git hash-object bin/rick` = `730a7db7...` (the current working file, which matches what's staged)\n\nSo my earlier `git add bin/rick` actually... wait, no. The first `git add bin/rick` failed with \"ignored\". But now it shows `M  bin/rick` (staged).\n\nOh! I see. The second time I ran `git add -v bin/rick`, it ALSO failed with \"ignored\". But now it's showing as staged \"M \".\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The `git add bin/rick` in my \"rebuild + commit\" attempt failed with exit 1 and \"ignored\". But then I ran `git add -v bin/rick` in the investigation, which ALSO failed.\n\nBut now `git status` shows `M  bin/rick` (staged). This means bin/rick IS staged. How?\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read. The `git status --short` output format:\n- `M  bin/rick` = \"M\" in column 1 (staged), space in column 2 (working tree clean relative to index)\n\nSo bin/rick is STAGED (modified in index). But I never successfully ran `git add bin/rick`...\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The `git add -v bin/rick` output was:\n```\nThe following paths are ignored by one of your .gitignore files:\nbin\n```\n\nBut it ALSO might have staged it anyway? No, git add doesn't stage ignored files.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. The error message says \"bin\" (the directory), and exit=1. But the file might still have been... no.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just check: is bin/rick actually staged right now? Let me check `git diff --cached --stat bin/rick`.\n\nActually, the git status shows `M  bin/rick` with \"M\" in the first column. That's definitely staged.\n\nWait, I bet what happened is: my FIRST `git add bin/rick` (in the commit attempt) actually... no, it returned exit 1.\n\nLet me just check the staged state directly and move on. The important thing is whether bin/rick should be committed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The hash comparison:\n- HEAD:bin/rick = `6e2a376c` \n- working bin/rick = `730a7db7`\n\nThese are DIFFERENT. So the working binary differs from HEAD. And it's staged (`M `). So I need to commit it.\n\nWait, but `git add bin/rick` said \"ignored\". This is the classic git behavior where `git add` on a path that matches gitignore BUT is already tracked... should still work. Unless the file was REMOVED from tracking.\n\nActually, let me look at this differently. The `git ls-files --stage bin/rick` earlier showed `100755 730a7db7... 0 bin/rick`. This means the INDEX has bin/rick with hash `730a7db7`. And `git rev-parse HEAD:bin/rick` = `6e2a376c`. So the index (staged) differs from HEAD, meaning bin/rick IS staged with a new version.\n\nSo somehow bin/rick got staged (with hash 730a7db7). Maybe the `git add -v bin/rick` DID stage it despite the \"ignored\" warning? Let me check: git add with `-v` on a tracked-but-ignored file...\n\nActually, you know what, this is a known git quirk. When a file is tracked AND matches a gitignore pattern, `git add` behaves inconsistently. But the bottom line from `git status` is clear: `M  bin/rick` is staged.\n\nWait, actually — I wonder if the \"ignored\" error is a RED HERRING. Let me check: maybe my `git add internal/prompt/templates/` earlier ALSO somehow staged bin/rick? No, I used `git add internal/prompt/templates/` specifically.\n\nUgh, let me just stop investigating and do the right thing: commit the staged bin/rick (if it's staged) or stage it with -f and commit.\n\nLet me check the staged state cleanly."}}
